    <h2>Free Online Image Format Converter</h2>
    <p>This tool converts images between JPG, PNG, and WebP, and also accepts GIF, BMP, SVG, and HEIC (iPhone photos) as source files. Add one or more images, pick your output format, adjust quality if needed, and tap Convert — your files are ready in seconds with no upload and no sign-up.</p>

    <h2>JPG vs PNG vs WebP — Which Should You Choose?</h2>
    <table class="fmt-table">
      <tr><th>Format</th><th>Best for</th><th>Transparency</th></tr>
      <tr><td>JPG</td><td>Photos, everyday sharing, maximum compatibility</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PNG</td><td>Screenshots, logos, graphics with sharp edges or text</td><td>Yes, lossless</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WebP</td><td>Websites — smaller files at similar quality to JPG/PNG</td><td>Yes, supported</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>JPG uses lossy compression and is understood by every device, browser, and printer, which makes it the safest general-purpose choice for photos. PNG is lossless, so it never loses detail, and it keeps transparent backgrounds intact — ideal for logos, icons, and screenshots with text. WebP was built for the web and typically produces smaller files than JPG or PNG at a similar visual quality, with optional transparency, though a few older tools still don't open it.</p>

    <h2>Supported Source Formats</h2>
    <table class="fmt-table">
      <tr><th>Format</th><th>Common source</th></tr>
      <tr><td>JPG</td><td>Photos, existing compressed images</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PNG</td><td>Screenshots, graphics with transparency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WebP</td><td>Modern websites, Google Images</td></tr>
      <tr><td>HEIC / HEIF</td><td>iPhone and iPad photos</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GIF</td><td>Animated images (first frame used)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>BMP</td><td>Windows Paint, older software</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SVG</td><td>Vector graphics, icons, logos</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h2>How the Conversion Works</h2>
    <p>Each image is drawn onto an in-memory canvas at its original size, or scaled down if you set a maximum dimension, then re-encoded in your chosen output format at the quality level you pick. JPG has no transparency channel, so if you convert to JPG, any transparent pixels are filled with your selected background color first; PNG and WebP keep transparency automatically. HEIC photos are decoded to a standard image format before this same process runs. Everything happens locally — your files are never sent to a server.</p>

    <h2>Fine-Tuning Your Output</h2>
    <p>For JPG and WebP, you can optionally target a specific file size in KB — useful when a website or form has an upload limit — and the converter automatically finds a quality level that gets close to it. PNG is lossless, so file size instead depends mainly on image content and dimensions. You can also add a custom file name prefix, applied to every download and to the ZIP.</p>

    <h2>Batch Conversion and Downloads</h2>
    <p>Add several images at once and convert them all to the same output format together, with live progress as each one finishes. Download each file individually, tap a thumbnail to compare the original against the converted version, copy a single result straight to your clipboard, or use "Download all (ZIP)" to get every converted file in one archive.</p>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <p><strong>Which formats can I convert between?</strong> You can add JPG, PNG, WebP, GIF, BMP, SVG, or HEIC images, and convert them to JPG, PNG, or WebP — whichever output format you choose.</p>
    <p><strong>What's the difference between JPG, PNG, and WebP?</strong> JPG is a compressed format best for photos and widely supported everywhere. PNG is lossless and supports transparency, best for graphics, logos, and screenshots with sharp edges. WebP offers strong compression with optional transparency and is well supported by modern browsers.</p>
    <p><strong>Does converting HEIC photos from my iPhone work?</strong> Yes. HEIC files are automatically decoded in your browser and can be converted to JPG, PNG, or WebP, so iPhone photos work without any extra app.</p>
    <p><strong>Are my images uploaded to a server?</strong> No. All conversion happens locally in your browser using the Canvas API. Your images never leave your device.</p>
    <p><strong>Why does a background color option appear for JPG?</strong> JPG does not support transparency. If your source image has transparent areas and you choose JPG as the output, they are filled with the background color you choose. PNG and WebP keep transparency automatically.</p>
    <p><strong>Can I target a specific file size?</strong> Yes, when converting to JPG or WebP. Turn on the target file size option and enter a size in KB. PNG is lossless, so this option doesn't apply to it.</p>
    <p><strong>Can I convert multiple images at once?</strong> Yes. You can add several images together, convert them all to the same output format in one click, and download them individually or as a single ZIP file.</p>
    <p><strong>Is this converter free?</strong> Yes, it is completely free with no sign-up required, and runs instantly in your browser.</p>

    <p class="disclaimer">
      <strong>Note:</strong> Very large images or a large batch may take a few extra seconds to process on older phones, since all conversion runs on your device rather than a server. Images are converted one at a time and automatically resized if needed, to keep memory use low and avoid crashes.
    </p>
